Specification Accounts

Description The chart of accounts, and what an account balance means.
Details SimpleAccount keeps one tree of accounts. The types that hold money and
\ the types Quicken calls categories are both nodes in that tree, which is
\ what allows every transaction to balance. See Transactions.
\
\ An account has no Name attribute. The name is the last segment of the
\ path, and is derived by the rule below rather than stored twice.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The account
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Entity Account
Description One node of the tree, category or real account alike. Which it is
\ follows from Type: Income, Expense and Equity are categories, and
\ everything else is a real account. One entity rather than two,
\ because almost everything about a node -- its name, where it sits,
\ whether it is hidden -- means the same thing either way; only Bank,
\ BankId and AccountNumber mean nothing on a category.
Uses AccountPath and AccountType from CoreTypes
| Attribute   | Type        | Default | Notes                               |
| Id          | AccountId   | none    | Identity; given once, never changes. See the identity rule below |
| Path        | AccountPath |         | Unique; what a person types and what every table here names an account by |
| Type        | AccountType | Checking | Fixed once the account has entries  |
| Placeholder | Boolean     | false   | A grouping node; takes no entries   |
| Hidden      | Boolean     | false   | Kept out of pickers, history kept   |
| Alias       | Text        | none    | A short way to type it; see finding a category |
| Bank        | Text        | none    | Which institution it is at; see what is kept about an account |
| BankId      | Text        | none    | What the institution calls itself in a download -- its routing number |
| AccountNumber | Text      | none    | The number on the statement, kept as written |
| DownloadedBalance | Money | 0.00 | What the last download said the balance was; written by the import, never typed |
| DownloadedBalanceAt | Text | never | The date that balance was for, or never |

Collection ChartOfAccounts
Description Every account in the book, in one tree.
| DataType | Minimum | Maximum | Notes          |
| Account  | 0       | 100000  | Path is unique |

BusinessRule The name of an account is the last segment of its path
Description A root account has no parent, so its parent is empty.
Examples: AccountNameDerivation
| Path                    | Name             | Parent        |
| Expenses                | Expenses         |               |
| Expenses:Auto           | Auto             | Expenses      |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel      | Fuel             | Expenses:Auto |
| Equity:Opening Balances | Opening Balances | Equity        |

Attributes AccountNameDerivation
| Attribute | Type        | Default |
| Path      | AccountPath |         |
| Name      | AccountName |         |
| Parent    | Text        | none    |

Background A small chart of accounts
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                    | Type       | Placeholder |
| Assets                  | Checking   | true        |
| Assets:Checking         | Checking   | false       |
| Assets:Savings          | Checking   | false       |
| Liabilities             | OtherDebt  | true        |
| Liabilities:Visa        | CreditCard | false       |
| Income                  | Income     | true        |
| Income:Salary           | Income     | false       |
| Expenses                | Expense    | true        |
| Expenses:Groceries      | Expense    | false       |
| Expenses:Auto           | Expense    | true        |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel      | Expense    | false       |
| Expenses:Auto:Repairs   | Expense    | false       |
| Equity:Opening Balances | Equity     | false       |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Creating accounts
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Scenario Adding an account under a parent
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
When account added : Account Vertical
| Path | Expenses:Auto:Insurance |
| Type | Expense                 |
Then account is : Account Vertical
| Path        | Expenses:Auto:Insurance |
| Type        | Expense                 |
| Placeholder | false                   |
| Hidden      | false                   |

Scenario A missing parent is created as a placeholder
Description Importing twenty years of data names deep categories that were
\ never created by hand, so the missing levels are filled in and
\ marked as placeholders.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
When account added : Account Vertical
| Path | Expenses:Home:Garden:Seeds |
| Type | Expense                    |
Then accounts created are : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                       | Type    | Placeholder |
| Expenses:Home              | Expense | true        |
| Expenses:Home:Garden       | Expense | true        |
| Expenses:Home:Garden:Seeds | Expense | false       |
# The first two were filled in; the third is the one that was asked for.

Scenario A filled-in parent takes the type of its child
Description A placeholder created on the way to an Expense is itself an Expense,
\ so the roll-up in a report adds up.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
When account added : Account Vertical
| Path | Income:Interest:Bank Interest |
| Type | Income                        |
Then accounts created are : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                          | Type   | Placeholder |
| Income:Interest               | Income | true        |
| Income:Interest:Bank Interest | Income | false       |

Scenario Two accounts may not share a path
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
When account added : Account Vertical
| Path | Assets:Checking |
| Type | Checking        |
Then rejected because : Rejection
| Reason                                  |
| An account named Assets:Checking exists |

Attributes Rejection
| Attribute | Type | Default |
| Reason    | Text |         |

Scenario The same name under two different parents is fine
Description Expenses:Auto:Insurance and Expenses:Home:Insurance are two
\ categories, and both are named Insurance.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
When account added : Account Vertical
| Path | Expenses:Auto:Insurance |
| Type | Expense                 |
When account added : Account Vertical
| Path | Expenses:Home:Insurance |
| Type | Expense                 |
Then accounts named Insurance are : ChartOfAccounts CompareOnly
| Path                    |
| Expenses:Auto:Insurance |
| Expenses:Home:Insurance |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Renaming and moving
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Scenario Renaming an account renames its descendants
Description The path of a child is built from the path of its parent, so a
\ rename rewrites every path beneath it. Transactions are untouched:
\ they refer to the account, not to the text of its path.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
When account renamed : AccountRename Vertical
| Path    | Expenses:Auto |
| NewName | Vehicle       |
Then accounts are : ChartOfAccounts CompareOnly
| Path                     |
| Expenses:Vehicle         |
| Expenses:Vehicle:Fuel    |
| Expenses:Vehicle:Repairs |

Attributes AccountRename
| Attribute | Type        | Default |
| Path      | AccountPath |         |
| NewName   | AccountName |         |

Scenario Moving an account to another parent
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
When account moved : AccountMove Vertical
| Path      | Expenses:Auto:Fuel |
| NewParent | Expenses           |
Then accounts are : ChartOfAccounts CompareOnly
| Path          |
| Expenses:Fuel |

Attributes AccountMove
| Attribute | Type        | Default |
| Path      | AccountPath |         |
| NewParent | AccountPath |         |

Scenario An account may not be moved beneath itself
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
When account moved : AccountMove Vertical
| Path      | Expenses:Auto      |
| NewParent | Expenses:Auto:Fuel |
Then rejected because : Rejection
| Reason                                   |
| An account may not be its own descendant |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deleting and hiding
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Scenario An account with entries is not deleted
Description Twenty years of history is the point of the program, so nothing
\ silently discards a transaction. Hide the account instead.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
Given Expenses:Groceries has entries : EntryCount
| Count |
| 412   |
When account deleted : AccountSelect
| Path               |
| Expenses:Groceries |
Then rejected because : Rejection
| Reason                                               |
| Expenses:Groceries has 412 postings; hide it instead |

Attributes EntryCount
Description How many transactions name an account, counting both sides.
| Attribute | Type    | Default |
| Count     | Integer | 0       |

Scenario An account with no entries is deleted
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
Given Expenses:Auto:Repairs has entries : EntryCount
| Count |
| 0     |
When account deleted : AccountSelect
| Path                  |
| Expenses:Auto:Repairs |
Then accounts under Expenses:Auto are : ChartOfAccounts CompareOnly
| Path               |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel |

Scenario Hiding an account keeps its history
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
When account hidden : AccountSelect
| Path               |
| Expenses:Groceries |
Then account is : Account Vertical
| Path        | Expenses:Groceries |
| Type        | Expense            |
| Placeholder | false              |
| Hidden      | true               |
Then category picker offers : ChartOfAccounts CompareOnly
| Path                  |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel    |
| Expenses:Auto:Repairs |
# Expenses:Groceries is absent from the picker, and its 412 transactions still
# report under it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What identifies an account
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

BusinessRule An account is identified by something that is not its path
Description The path is a label. It is what people read, what they type and what
\ every table in this specification names an account by, and it can
\ change: a category can be renamed, and it can be moved under a
\ different parent. What identifies the account does not change when
\ either of those happens.
Details The identifier is a plain, incrementing number, given once when the
\ account is created. Not the millisecond-clock scheme a transaction's
\ Id uses: an account is created rarely enough, and always by a person
\ rather than an import running flat out, that nothing cleverer than a
\ counter is needed to keep two of them from colliding.
\
\ It is deliberately absent from every table in this specification. Nobody
\ types it, nobody reads it, and a scenario that stated one would be
\ stating something no scenario controls. Accounts are named here by path,
\ which is what a reader understands.
\
\ What this buys is that rearranging categories is a small operation. With
\ the path as the identity, moving Auto:Fuel under Vehicle means rewriting
\ every transaction that named it -- tens of thousands of rows for one
\ decision, and a book that refers to a category that no longer exists if
\ it stops half way. With the path derived from the tree, the same move
\ changes which parent the account points at, and the paths are worked out
\ again afterwards. No transaction is touched, because no transaction ever named
\ the path in the first place.
Examples: AccountRearranged
| WhatChanges              | ThePath | TheIdentity | ThePostings |
| Renaming a category      | Yes     | No          | Untouched   |
| Moving it under another  | Yes     | No          | Untouched   |
| Hiding it                | No      | No          | Untouched   |
| Changing its type        | No      | No          | Untouched   |

Attributes AccountRearranged
| Attribute    | Type   | Default |
| WhatChanges  | String |         |
| ThePath      | Text   | No      |
| TheIdentity  | Text   | No      |
| ThePostings  | Text   | Untouched |

Scenario Moving a category under another parent leaves its transactions alone
Description The transactions named the account, not the path, so moving it is a
\ change to one account and to nothing else. The paths underneath are
\ worked out again, which is why Fuel reads as Vehicle:Fuel
\ afterwards without anything having rewritten it.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Auto          | Expense | true        |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel     | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Vehicle       | Expense | true        |
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Destination        | Amount |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | Expenses:Auto:Fuel | -40.00 |
When account moved under : AccountMove Vertical
| Path      | Expenses:Auto:Fuel |
| NewParent | Expenses:Vehicle   |
Then the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts CompareOnly
| Path                  |
| Expenses:Vehicle:Fuel |
Then balances are : AccountBalance CompareOnly
| Account               | RawBalance |
| Expenses:Vehicle:Fuel | 40.00      |
# The forty pounds did not move and was never rewritten: the transaction names the
# account, and the account is the same account.

Attributes AccountMove
| Attribute | Type        | Default |
| Path      | AccountPath |         |
| NewParent | AccountPath |         |

DataType AccountId
Description The identity an account is given once, when it is created, and
\ never afterwards. See the identity rule above.
Uses Integer
Examples: ValidValues
| Value | IsValid | Notes                           |
| 1     | true    | The first account ever created  |
| 412   | true    | The program assigns the number  |
|       | false   | An account always has one       |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Accounts and categories are separate entities
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Description Account and Category are redone here as two separate entities
\ rather than one Account entity that Type happens to make a
\ category of. They keep almost nothing in common: a category has no
\ bank and no account number, and is offered by typing rather than
\ read off a statement, while an account is never found by typing
\ part of its name. Keeping them apart is what "different data"
\ means in practice.
Details Nothing below removes the Account entity above, and nothing below
\ changes what a ChartOfAccounts table in an existing scenario looks
\ like: Path stays what every table in this specification names a node
\ by, for both kinds, the same as it always has. What changes is what a
\ node is identified and stored by underneath that convenience, which
\ these new rules say and the existing ones did not have to.

DataType CategoryId
Description The identity a category is given once, when it is created, and
\ never afterwards. Drawn from the same counter as AccountId: see
\ the rule below. Zero is "none".
Uses Integer
Examples: ValidValues
| Value | IsValid | Notes                               |
| 2     | true    | The second id the counter gave out  |
| 413   | true    |                                     |
|       | false   | A category always has one           |

BusinessRule An account id and a category id never collide
Description Accounts and categories are two separate collections, but one
\ number line between them: the counter that hands out an AccountId
\ is the same counter that hands out a CategoryId, so a program
\ handed a bare number and told "look this up" never has to be told
\ which collection to look in first, and never guesses wrong.
Details This is what makes it safe for a transaction or a split line to name "the
\ other side" by one id with no separate flag saying which kind it is.
\ See Transactions.
Examples: IdsGivenOut
| CreatedInOrder       | Kind     | Id | Notes                        |
| First account made   | Account  | 1  |                              |
| First category made  | Category | 2  | Not 1: the counter is shared |
| Second account made   | Account  | 3  |                              |
| Second category made  | Category | 4  |                              |

Attributes IdsGivenOut
| Attribute      | Type    | Default |
| CreatedInOrder | String  |         |
| Kind           | String  |         |
| Id             | Integer | 0       |
| Notes          | String  | none    |

BusinessRule An id is never given out a second time
Description The counter only goes forward. The book records the last id it
\ handed out, and the next id is that number plus one, so deleting
\ an account does not free its number for the next one created.
Details Working the next id out from the largest one in use would hand a
\ deleted account's number to the next account made, and anything still
\ naming the old one -- a transaction in a file not yet imported, a
\ report somebody saved, a backup opened a year later -- would read as
\ naming the new one. The counter is recorded, not computed. Where it is
\ recorded is in Books.
\ Each row below creates one node, so the group is made first and the
\ accounts under it afterwards. A path whose parent is missing creates
\ that parent as well, and takes an id for it too -- which is the adding
\ rule above, not this one.
Examples: CounterOnlyGoesForward
| WhatHappens                     | LastIdUsed | NextIdGivenOut |
| A new book, nothing created yet | 0          | 1              |
| Assets created                  | 1          | 2              |
| Assets:Checking created         | 2          | 3              |
| Assets:Checking deleted         | 2          | 3              |
| Assets:Savings created          | 3          | 4              |

Attributes CounterOnlyGoesForward
| Attribute      | Type    | Default |
| WhatHappens    | String  |         |
| LastIdUsed     | Integer | 0       |
| NextIdGivenOut | Integer | 0       |

Entity Category
Description A node Quicken calls a category -- Income, Expense or Equity --
\ kept apart from Account because almost nothing else about it is
\ shared. See the business rule above for why its id still cannot
\ collide with an account's.
Uses AccountPath and AccountType from CoreTypes
| Attribute     | Type        | Default | Notes                               |
| Id            | CategoryId  | none    | Identity; given once, never changes |
| SubcategoryOf | CategoryId  | 0       | 0 is top-level; otherwise the category it sits under |
| Path          | AccountPath |         | Unique; derived by walking SubcategoryOf and Name when the record is read, not stored as text itself |
| Type          | AccountType | Expense | Income, Expense or Equity           |
| Placeholder   | Boolean     | false   | A grouping node; takes no entries    |
| Hidden        | Boolean     | false   | Kept out of pickers, history kept    |
| Alias         | Text        | none    | A short way to type it; see finding a category |

Collection Categories
Description Every category in the book, in one tree, separate from Accounts.
| DataType | Minimum | Maximum | Notes          |
| Category | 0       | 100000  | Path is unique |

Attributes CategoryRow
Description A raw row as it would be read from storage, before its path is
\ derived -- named by id and parent id, the way the database holds
\ it, rather than by the path a person types.
| Attribute     | Type        | Default |
| Id            | Integer     | 0       |
| SubcategoryOf | Integer     | 0       |
| Name          | AccountName |         |
| Type          | AccountType | Expense |

Scenario A category's path is derived from its parent chain when it is read
Description Nothing stores Expenses:Auto:Fuel as text. SubcategoryOf points at
\ the category above, and the path is walked out from that, the same
\ way an account's path already is worked out from the tree for
\ display. See the identity rule above.
Given raw category rows are : CategoryRow
| Id | SubcategoryOf | Name     | Type    |
| 1  | 0              | Expenses | Expense |
| 2  | 1              | Auto     | Expense |
| 3  | 2              | Fuel     | Expense |
When the rows are read as categories
Then categories are : Category CompareOnly
| Path               |
| Expenses           |
| Expenses:Auto      |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Showing an account or a category as one line of text
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

BusinessRule Converting an account to the one line it is shown as
Description Everywhere a payee rule, a register's category column, or a
\ category picker needs one piece of text for an account or a
\ category, it is built the same way and it is never stored: it is
\ worked out from the tree, the moment it is needed, the same as a
\ path is.
Details A category shows as its name, and the name of everything between it
\ and its section, joined with colons -- Auto:Fuel, not
\ Expenses:Auto:Fuel. The section itself is never part of the text: it is
\ shown afterwards, in parentheses, which is the Quicken convention these
\ reports are checked against. Two categories that read the same but sit
\ in different sections -- an Auto under Income and one under Expenses,
\ unlikely as that is -- are told apart by it.
\
\ A real account shows as its own name in square brackets, with nothing
\ of its ancestors in it: a card under no particular group reads
\ [Visa], not [Liabilities:Visa]. This is the bracket convention the
\ register already uses for the other side of a transfer; converting an
\ account is how every other place that needs the same text gets it.
\
\ Placeholder is not part of this: a placeholder is converted exactly
\ the same way as any other node, because something still has to be
\ shown for it on a picker that lets a parent be chosen too.
Examples: AccountDisplay
| Path                  | Type     | ShownAs           |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel     | Expense  | Auto:Fuel (Expense) |
| Income:Salary          | Income   | Salary (Income)     |
| Expenses:Auto          | Expense  | Auto (Expense)       |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking | [Checking]           |
| Liabilities:Visa       | CreditCard | [Visa]             |

Attributes AccountDisplay
| Attribute | Type        | Default |
| Path      | AccountPath |         |
| Type      | AccountType |         |
| ShownAs   | Text        |         |

Scenario Converting every account at once is cheap enough to repeat on every change
Description A few hundred accounts and categories at most, so converting the
\ whole chart over again -- rather than patching the one that moved --
\ is simpler and still instant. This runs when the book is opened,
\ and again after any add, edit, move, rename, hide or delete of
\ either an account or a category.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Auto          | Expense | true        |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel     | Expense | false       |
When the display names are rebuilt
Then accounts show as : AccountDisplay CompareOnly
| Path               | ShownAs              |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel | Auto:Fuel (Expense)  |
| Assets:Checking    | [Checking]           |
# Renaming Fuel to Petrol and rebuilding again would change only the first of
# these two answers -- to Auto:Petrol (Expense) -- and nothing else, because
# nothing else is downstream of the one node that changed.

Scenario A category's display name is rebuilt the same way, and when the book opens
Description Category is now a separate entity from Account, but the rebuild is
\ one pass over everything either entity holds, not two: a book with
\ a few hundred nodes between them converts all of them, every time
\ it is opened and every time one is added, edited, moved, renamed,
\ hidden or deleted, and it is still instant.
Given the categories are : Categories
| Path               | Placeholder |
| Expenses:Auto      | true        |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel | false       |
When the book is reopened
Then categories show as : AccountDisplay CompareOnly
| Path               | ShownAs             |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel | Auto:Fuel (Expense) |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What is kept about an account
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Description Each account has a dialog of its own, holding what is known about
\ the account itself: which bank it is at, what that bank calls itself
\ in a download, and the number on the statement.
Details These are facts about the account and not about any transaction, so
\ they live on the account rather than in a setting shared by the book.
\ Two current accounts at the same bank have to be told apart, and the
\ number is how the statement tells them apart.
\
\ More will go here. The dialog is the place for anything true of one
\ account and of nothing else -- which CSV profile its downloads are in,
\ what its statements call a payment to it -- which is why it is a
\ dialog per account rather than another column in the account list.
\
\ An OFX download names an account in the bank's own terms, a routing
\ number and an account number. Those are these: the three of them sit
\ on the account together, and matching a download to an account is
\ reading them off the account it belongs to. There is no second place
\ where a bank's numbering is recorded, because a second place is a
\ second thing to keep in step, and the one thing a download has to
\ find is an account.
\
\ Bank is for a person and BankId is for a file, which is why they are
\ two attributes rather than one. Nobody should have to type a routing
\ number to label an account, and nothing reads "First Bank" out of a
\ download. Neither is ever required: an account with none of this works
\ exactly as it always did, and a QIF history carries none of it, so
\ every account migrated from Quicken starts with all three empty. The
\ first download into such an account is where they get filled in; see
\ what a download teaches an account in ImportOfx.

Attributes AccountSettings
Description What the dialog of one account holds.
| Attribute       | Type        | Default    |
| Path            | AccountPath |            |
| Bank            | Text        | none       |
| BankId          | Text        | none       |
| AccountNumber   | Text        | none       |
| StartingBalance | Money       | 0.00       |
| StartingDate    | Date        | 2005-01-01 |

Scenario An account starts with nothing known about it but its name
Description Nothing is required. An account with no bank and no number works
\ exactly as it does now, and the dialog is somewhere to put the
\ answer when there is one.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path            | Type | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking | Checking | false       |
When account settings opened : AccountSelect
| Path            |
| Assets:Checking |
Then account settings are : AccountSettings Vertical
| Path          | Assets:Checking |
| Bank          | none            |
| BankId        | none            |
| AccountNumber | none            |

# All three are the account's, so all three are written with it.
Scenario The bank and the number are kept with the book
Description Written when they are entered, because a book is a file and not a
\ session. See the saving rule in Books.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path            | Type | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking | Checking | false       |
When account settings changed : AccountSettings Vertical
| Path          | Assets:Checking |
| Bank          | First Bank      |
| BankId        | 053000196       |
| AccountNumber | 1234567890      |
When the book is reopened
Then account settings are : AccountSettings Vertical
| Path          | Assets:Checking |
| Bank          | First Bank      |
| BankId        | 053000196       |
| AccountNumber | 1234567890      |
# All three are the account's, so all three are written with it. The routing
# number is what the next download will be matched on, and a download that
# had to ask again every month would be asking for something the book already
# knew.

Scenario An account number is kept as it is written
Description A number on a statement is a label and not an amount: it may carry
\ dashes or spaces, it may have a leading zero that matters, and it
\ is never added to anything. So it is kept exactly as typed, with
\ only the blanks at either end taken off.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path             | Type       | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking  | Checking   | false       |
| Liabilities:Visa | CreditCard | false       |
When account settings changed : AccountSettings Vertical
| Path          | Assets:Checking |
| Bank          | First Bank      |
| AccountNumber | 0012-3456       |
When account settings changed : AccountSettings Vertical
| Path          | Liabilities:Visa    |
| Bank          | Second Bank         |
| AccountNumber | ~4111 1111 1111 1111 |
Then account settings are : AccountSettings
| Path             | Bank        | AccountNumber       |
| Assets:Checking  | First Bank  | 0012-3456           |
| Liabilities:Visa | Second Bank | 4111 1111 1111 1111 |
# The tilde in the table is a space, so the second number is written with a
# leading blank and is kept without it. The leading zeros of the first survive,
# which they would not if a number were read as a number.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What is kept about a category
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Description A category has a dialog of its own, and it asks what the dialog
\ that adds one asks: the name, what it sits under, which kind it is,
\ the short way to type it, and whether it is kept out of the way.
Details It held the alias and nothing else to begin with, which made it the
\ one editor in the program that could not correct what a person had
\ got wrong. A category entered under the wrong parent, or named badly,
\ had to be added again in the right place and the old one hidden --
\ and the transactions stayed on the old one, so the mistake outlived
\ the fix.
\
\ Renaming and moving are the chart's own operations and are specified
\ in the sections above: a rename rewrites the paths beneath it, a move
\ carries the subtree, and the transactions on a category are untouched
\ by either, because a transaction names the category and not the text
\ of its path. The dialog is where they are reached for a category,
\ which was the only kind of node with no way to reach them.
\
\ Type is asked because a category entered as an expense when it was
\ income reads as the wrong sign on every report that covers it. It is
\ the same question the adding dialog asks, so it gets the same answer
\ and the same list of three.

Attributes CategorySettings
Description What the dialog of one category holds. Path is what it was opened
\ on; the rest is what it comes up showing, and what it writes back
\ when it is changed.
| Attribute | Type        | Default |
| Path      | AccountPath |         |
| Name      | AccountName | none    |
| Under     | AccountPath | none    |
| Type      | AccountType | Expense |
| Alias     | Text        | none    |
| Hidden    | Boolean     | false   |

Scenario The category dialog comes up holding everything the category has
Description Opened on a category, it shows the five answers rather than one.
\ Under is the parent it sits beneath, which is the path with the name
\ taken off.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type    | Placeholder |
| Expenses:Auto      | Expense | true        |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel | Expense | false       |
When category settings opened : AccountSelect
| Path               |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel |
Then category settings are : CategorySettings Vertical
| Path   | Expenses:Auto:Fuel |
| Name   | Fuel               |
| Under  | Expenses:Auto      |
| Type   | Expense            |
| Alias  | none               |
| Hidden | false              |

Scenario A category renamed from its dialog renames what is under it
Description The chart's rename, reached from the dialog. Everything beneath it
\ follows, because a child's path is built from its parent's.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                 | Type    | Placeholder |
| Expenses             | Expense | true        |
| Expenses:Auto        | Expense | true        |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel   | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Auto:Repair | Expense | false       |
When category settings changed : CategorySettings Vertical
| Path   | Expenses:Auto |
| Name   | Vehicle       |
| Under  | Expenses      |
| Type   | Expense       |
| Alias  | none          |
| Hidden | false         |
Then the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts CompareOnly
| Path                    |
| Expenses:Vehicle        |
| Expenses:Vehicle:Fuel   |
| Expenses:Vehicle:Repair |

Scenario A category moved from its dialog takes its children with it
Description Changing Under is the chart's move. The name is unchanged, so the
\ subtree arrives under the new parent reading as it did before.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                 | Type    | Placeholder |
| Expenses             | Expense | true        |
| Expenses:Auto        | Expense | true        |
| Expenses:Auto:Repair | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Household   | Expense | true        |
When category settings changed : CategorySettings Vertical
| Path   | Expenses:Auto      |
