| Hidden | false              |
Then category settings are : CategorySettings Vertical
| Path   | Expenses:Auto:Fuel |
| Name   | Fuel               |
| Under  | Expenses:Auto      |
| Type   | Expense            |
| Alias  | @fuel             |
| Hidden | false              |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The starting balance, in the dialog rather than only at creation
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Description The dialog shows what the account starts from -- an amount and the
\ date it starts on -- and both can be set there, not only in the
\ moment the account is created.
Details There is still no opening balance field on an account. What the dialog
\ shows is the transaction against Equity:Opening Balances, read back
\ off the book, and editing the field edits that transaction. So the
\ book balances from its first day whichever way the figure was
\ entered, and the figure lives in one place rather than in two that
\ would have to be kept in step. See the opening balances section
\ above, which is the decision this follows.
\
\ An account that came from a QIF history already has one, posted by
\ the import. An account typed in by hand has none, and the dialog is
\ where that is put right: the usual reason is a statement from 2005
\ in a drawer, and the usual motive is wanting the register to
\ reconcile.
\
\ A starting balance of zero is no starting balance, as it is at
\ creation, and the date beside it then says nothing. Reading a date
\ out of a dialog that has no amount to go with it is not worth
\ asking after, so the scenarios that have no balance do not care
\ what it says.

Scenario The dialog shows the starting balance the account was given
Description Read off the transaction rather than out of a field, because the
\ transaction is where it is.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount  | Date       | Destination             | Amount  |
| T1  | Assets:Savings | 2005-01-01 | Equity:Opening Balances | 1200.00 |
When account settings opened : AccountSelect
| Path           |
| Assets:Savings |
Then account settings are : AccountSettings Vertical
| Path            | Assets:Savings |
| StartingBalance | 1200.00        |
| StartingDate    | 2005-01-01     |

Scenario An account with no starting balance shows none
Description Most accounts in a book imported from Quicken have one. An account
\ typed in afterwards does not, and the dialog says so rather than
\ showing a figure nobody entered.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
When account settings opened : AccountSelect
| Path            |
| Assets:Checking |
Then account settings are : AccountSettings Vertical
| Path            | Assets:Checking |
| StartingBalance | 0.00            |
| StartingDate    | ?DNC?           |

Scenario A starting balance entered in the dialog posts the opening transaction
Description The account was typed in with nothing in it and the statement it
\ starts from turned up later.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
When account settings changed : AccountSettings Vertical
| Path            | Assets:Savings |
| StartingBalance | 1200.00        |
| StartingDate    | 2005-01-01     |
Then balances are : AccountBalance
| Account                 | RawBalance | DisplayBalance | Notes                  |
| Assets:Savings          | 1200.00    | 1200.00        | What it started from   |
| Equity:Opening Balances | -1200.00   | 1200.00        | The other side of it   |

Scenario Changing the starting balance changes the transaction it already has
Description Not a second one. The dialog is editing the transaction it showed,
\ which is the whole reason the figure is not kept twice.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount  | Date       | Destination             | Amount  |
| T1  | Assets:Savings | 2005-01-01 | Equity:Opening Balances | 1200.00 |
When account settings changed : AccountSettings Vertical
| Path            | Assets:Savings |
| StartingBalance | 1450.00        |
| StartingDate    | 2005-01-01     |
Then balances are : AccountBalance
| Account        | RawBalance | DisplayBalance | Notes                            |
| Assets:Savings | 1450.00    | 1450.00        | The figure as changed, not added |
# A second transaction would read 2650.00 here, so the balance is the whole of
# what has to be checked: there is nothing a duplicate could hide behind.

Scenario The date a balance starts from can be changed on its own
Description The amount was right and the date was a guess, which is what
\ happens when a statement is found after the account was opened.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount  | Date       | Destination             | Amount  |
| T1  | Assets:Savings | 2005-01-01 | Equity:Opening Balances | 1200.00 |
When account settings changed : AccountSettings Vertical
| Path            | Assets:Savings |
| StartingBalance | 1200.00        |
| StartingDate    | 2004-07-31     |
When account settings opened : AccountSelect
| Path           |
| Assets:Savings |
Then account settings are : AccountSettings Vertical
| Path            | Assets:Savings |
| StartingBalance | 1200.00        |
| StartingDate    | 2004-07-31     |

Scenario A starting balance cleared to zero takes its transaction away
Description Zero is no starting balance, so what is left is an account with
\ none -- not an account with a transaction for nothing.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount  | Date       | Destination             | Amount  |
| T1  | Assets:Savings | 2005-01-01 | Equity:Opening Balances | 1200.00 |
When account settings changed : AccountSettings Vertical
| Path            | Assets:Savings |
| StartingBalance | 0.00           |
| StartingDate    | 2005-01-01     |
Then balances are : AccountBalance
| Account                 | RawBalance | DisplayBalance | Notes                  |
| Assets:Savings          | 0.00       | 0.00           | Nothing to start from  |
| Equity:Opening Balances | 0.00       | 0.00           | And nothing owed to it |
# Both sides, because taking one of them away and leaving the other is what
# going at this with a delete rather than with a transaction would do.

Scenario A card's starting balance is entered the way the statement reads
Description As an amount owed, like the one entered when a card is created,
\ and shown back the same way. A person reading a card statement is
\ not thinking in signs.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
When account settings changed : AccountSettings Vertical
| Path            | Liabilities:Visa |
| StartingBalance | 300.00           |
| StartingDate    | 2005-01-01       |
When account settings opened : AccountSelect
| Path             |
| Liabilities:Visa |
Then account settings are : AccountSettings Vertical
| Path            | Liabilities:Visa |
| StartingBalance | 300.00           |
| StartingDate    | 2005-01-01       |
Then balances are : AccountBalance
| Account          | RawBalance | DisplayBalance | Notes            |
| Liabilities:Visa | -300.00    | 300.00         | Owed, as entered |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Finding a category
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

BusinessRule Typing part of a category offers it and everything under it
Description Categorising a transaction by hand starts with typing. Whatever is
\ typed is matched against every part of every path, and a category
\ that matches is offered along with all the categories beneath it --
\ because typing a parent is how a person says "somewhere in here".
Details Matching is on any segment, not only the first, so "fuel" finds
\ Expenses:Auto:Fuel without anyone having typed Expenses or Auto. It
\ ignores case, because nobody capitalises while typing fast.
\
\ Offering the children of a match is the part that matters. There are
\ nearly four hundred categories here and the useful ones sit two levels
\ down; a picker that offered only exact matches would mean typing the
\ whole path, and one that offered only leaves would make a parent
\ untypeable.
\
\ A hidden category is not offered. Nor is one that has not been used for
\ years: the book has categories last used in 2008, and a list that offers
\ them alongside the ones in weekly use is a list nobody reads to the
\ bottom. Both stay in the book and both keep their history -- this is
\ about what is offered while typing, not about what exists.
Examples: CategoryMatch
| Typed | Offered                                            | Notes                                  |
| fuel  | Expenses:Auto:Fuel                                 | Any segment, not just the first        |
| FUEL  | Expenses:Auto:Fuel                                 | Case is ignored                        |
| auto  | Expenses:Auto, and every category under it         | Typing a parent means somewhere in here |
| @FU   | Expenses:Auto:Fuel                                 | An alias stands for one category        |
| zzz   | Nothing                                            | No match is not an error               |

Attributes CategoryMatch
| Attribute | Type        | Default |
| Typed     | SearchText  |         |
| Offered   | String      |         |
| Notes     | String      | none    |

Scenario Typing part of a name offers the category and its children
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                    | Type    | Placeholder | Hidden |
| Expenses:Auto           | Expense | true        | false  |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel      | Expense | false       | false  |
| Expenses:Auto:Insurance | Expense | false       | false  |
| Expenses:Groceries      | Expense | false       | false  |
When category typed : CategorySearch Vertical
| Typed | auto |
Then categories offered are : CategoryOffered
| Path                    |
| Expenses:Auto           |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel      |
| Expenses:Auto:Insurance |
# Groceries is not offered: nothing in its path matches.

Attributes CategorySearch
Description What has been typed so far, and whether the search was asked to
\ include the categories it normally leaves out.
| Attribute      | Type       | Default |
| Typed          | SearchText |         |
| IncludeUnused  | Boolean    | false   |

Attributes CategoryOffered
| Attribute | Type        | Default |
| Path      | AccountPath |         |

Scenario An alias stands for one category however little is typed
Description A category used weekly should not need its name typed. The alias is
\ the user's own, a couple of letters after an at sign, and it offers
\ exactly the one category rather than everything that matches.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                    | Type    | Placeholder | Alias |
| Expenses:Auto           | Expense | true        | none  |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel      | Expense | false       | @FU   |
| Expenses:Auto:Insurance | Expense | false       | none  |
When category typed : CategorySearch Vertical
| Typed | @FU |
Then categories offered are : CategoryOffered
| Path               |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel |
# Only the one. An alias is a person saying exactly which category they mean,
# so the children are not brought along as they are for a typed parent.

Scenario A category not used for years is left out while typing
Description It is still in the book, still has its history, and still appears in
\ a report that covers the years it was used. It is simply not put in
\ front of someone categorising a transaction today.
Given today is : Today Vertical
| Date | 2026-10-05 |
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Expenses:Auto          | Expense | true        |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel     | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Auto:Carriage | Expense | false       |
Given dated entries are : DatedAccountEntry
| Date       | Account                | Amount |
| 2026-09-01 | Expenses:Auto:Fuel     | 40.00  |
| 2009-04-02 | Expenses:Auto:Carriage | 12.00  |
When category typed : CategorySearch Vertical
| Typed | auto |
Then categories offered are : CategoryOffered
| Path               |
| Expenses:Auto      |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel |
# Carriage last saw a transaction in 2009, so it is not offered. Asking to see
# everything brings it back.

Scenario Asking for the unused ones brings them back
Given today is : Today Vertical
| Date | 2026-10-05 |
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Expenses:Auto          | Expense | true        |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel     | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Auto:Carriage | Expense | false       |
Given dated entries are : DatedAccountEntry
| Date       | Account                | Amount |
| 2026-09-01 | Expenses:Auto:Fuel     | 40.00  |
| 2009-04-02 | Expenses:Auto:Carriage | 12.00  |
When category typed : CategorySearch Vertical
| Typed         | auto |
| IncludeUnused | true |
Then categories offered are : CategoryOffered
| Path                   |
| Expenses:Auto          |
| Expenses:Auto:Carriage |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel     |
# Which is the point of it being a search and not a deletion.

Attributes LastUsed
Description The most recent date anything posted to one account. Never is a
\ word rather than a date, because an account nobody has posted to is
\ not the same thing as one used long ago -- see the rule above, which
\ offers the first and hides the second.
| Attribute | Type        | Default |
| Account   | AccountPath |         |
| LastUsed  | Text        | never   |

Scenario The date each account was last used is read in one pass over the book
Description Offering categories needs the last-used date of every candidate,
\ because a category nobody has posted to for two years is left out.
\ Asked one account at a time, each answer is a scan of the whole
\ book, so offering from a chart of five hundred accounts reads the
\ book five hundred times -- which on a book of thirty thousand
\ transactions is why the dialog took seconds to open and why every
\ keystroke took as long again.
Details So the dates are read together, all of them, in one pass over the
\ postings, and what is offered reads its answer out of that. Opening
\ the dialog is one pass; a keystroke is none, because the dates
\ cannot have changed while somebody is typing.
\
\ What is offered does not change by a line. This says only that the
\ same answer is arrived at once rather than once per account, which
\ is why the scenarios above are untouched and still pass.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type     | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel | Expense  | false       |
| Expenses:Dentist   | Expense  | false       |
| Expenses:Postage   | Expense  | false       |
Given dated entries are : DatedAccountEntry
| Date       | Account            | Amount |
| 2024-03-01 | Expenses:Auto:Fuel | 40.00  |
| 2026-02-01 | Expenses:Auto:Fuel | 44.00  |
| 2019-05-06 | Expenses:Dentist   | 90.00  |
When the last used dates are read
Then last used dates are : LastUsed
| Account            | LastUsed   |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel | 2026-02-01 |
| Expenses:Dentist   | 2019-05-06 |
| Expenses:Postage   | never      |
# The latest of the two fuel entries, not the first one read and not the last.
# Postage is in the chart and in no entry, which is the case that has to be
# told apart from a date.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What a balance is
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

DomainTerm RawBalance : Money
Description The sum of the amounts of every transaction to one account, with the
\ sign those transactions carry. Debits are positive throughout.

DomainTerm DisplayBalance : Money
Description The raw balance multiplied by the display sign of the account type,
\ so that each account reads the way its own statement reads.

BusinessRule Display sign follows from account type
Description Which account types read the other way round from their transactions.
Details A charge to a credit card credits that account, leaving a negative raw
\ balance, but the cardholder thinks of it as an amount owed. Income is
\ credited by a paycheque, and a report should show it as earned.
Examples: DisplaySignOf
| AccountType        | DisplaySign | Notes                                       |
| Checking           | 1           | Positive raw balance means money held       |
| Savings            | 1           |                                             |
| Cash               | 1           |                                             |
| Brokerage          | 1           |                                             |
| IRA                | 1           |                                             |
| HSA                | 1           |                                             |
| Retirement401k     | 1           |                                             |
| RealEstate         | 1           |                                             |
| Vehicle            | 1           |                                             |
| OtherAsset         | 1           |                                             |
| AccountsReceivable | 1           | Owed to the business, so money held         |
| TransferIn         | 1           | Off the balance sheet, and still money held |
| Expense            | 1           | Positive raw balance means money spent      |
| CreditCard         | -1          | Shown as the amount owed                    |
| LineOfCredit       | -1          |                                             |
| Mortgage           | -1          |                                             |
| AutoLoan           | -1          |                                             |
| PersonalLoan       | -1          |                                             |
| OtherDebt          | -1          | Shown as the amount owed                    |
| AccountsPayable    | -1          | Owed by the business                        |
| TransferOut        | -1          |                                             |
| Income             | -1          | Shown as the amount earned                  |
| Equity             | -1          | Shown as the amount contributed to the book |
# The sign is the balance side, for everything but the three categories: what
# is owned reads positive and what is owed reads as the amount owed. So there
# is one rule behind two tables, and the balance-side rule in CoreTypes is it.

Attributes DisplaySignOf
| Attribute   | Type        | Default |
| AccountType | AccountType | Checking |
| DisplaySign | Integer     | 1       |
| Notes       | String      | none    |

Define SomeEntries
Description The entries the next three scenarios all read, named so that each
\ can ask for them. A scenario cannot take a table from the scenario
\ before it: every generated test stands alone.
| Account            | Amount   |
| Assets:Checking    | 1000.00  |
| Assets:Checking    | -45.00   |
| Liabilities:Visa   | -120.00  |
| Liabilities:Visa   | 20.00    |
| Income:Salary      | -1000.00 |
| Expenses:Groceries | 45.00    |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel | 100.00   |

Scenario Balances of an account after some entries
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
Given entries are : AccountEntry
=SomeEntries
Then balances are : AccountBalance
| Account            | RawBalance | DisplayBalance | Notes            |
| Assets:Checking    | 955.00     | 955.00         | Money held       |
| Liabilities:Visa   | -100.00    | 100.00         | Owed on the card |
| Income:Salary      | -1000.00   | 1000.00        | Earned           |
| Expenses:Groceries | 45.00      | 45.00          | Spent            |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel | 100.00     | 100.00         | Spent            |

Attributes AccountEntry
Description An amount a transaction put against this account, from whichever
\ side of it. Used where a scenario is about what an account holds --
\ its balance, its rollup, its place in net worth -- and the other
\ side of each transaction is beside the point.
| Attribute | Type        | Default |
| Account   | AccountPath |         |
| Amount    | Money       | 0.00    |

Attributes AccountBalance
| Attribute      | Type        | Default |
| Account        | AccountPath |         |
| RawBalance     | Money       | 0.00    |
| DisplayBalance | Money       | 0.00    |
| Notes          | String      | none    |

Scenario A placeholder balance is the total of its descendants
Description A placeholder takes no transactions of its own, so its balance is
\ entirely the roll-up. A node that is both posted to and a parent
\ reports both numbers, which is why there are two columns.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
Given entries are : AccountEntry
=SomeEntries
Then rolled up balances are : RolledUpBalance
| Account            | OwnBalance | TotalBalance | Notes                       |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel | 100.00     | 100.00       | A leaf                      |
| Expenses:Auto      | 0.00       | 100.00       | Placeholder: all from below |
| Expenses:Groceries | 45.00      | 45.00        |                             |
| Expenses           | 0.00       | 145.00       | Groceries and Auto          |
| Assets             | 0.00       | 955.00       |                             |

Attributes RolledUpBalance
| Attribute    | Type        | Default |
| Account      | AccountPath |         |
| OwnBalance   | Money       | 0.00    |
| TotalBalance | Money       | 0.00    |
| Notes        | String      | none    |

Scenario Net worth is the total of the real accounts
Description The nominal accounts are left out: including them would always give
\ zero, because every transaction balances.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
Given entries are : AccountEntry
=SomeEntries
Then net worth is : NetWorth Vertical
| Assets      | 955.00 |
| Liabilities | 100.00 |
| NetWorth    | 855.00 |

Attributes NetWorth
| Attribute   | Type  | Default |
| Assets      | Money | 0.00    |
| Liabilities | Money | 0.00    |
| NetWorth    | Money | 0.00    |

Scenario A balance as at a date ignores later entries
Description Every balance in the program is a balance as at a date; the figure
\ shown without one is the balance as at today. See Reports.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
Given dated entries are : DatedAccountEntry
| Date       | Account         | Amount  |
| 2024-01-05 | Assets:Checking | 1000.00 |
| 2024-02-10 | Assets:Checking | -45.00  |
| 2024-03-20 | Assets:Checking | -55.00  |
Then balance as at dates are : BalanceAsAt
| AsAt       | Account         | Balance | Notes                       |
| 2024-01-04 | Assets:Checking | 0.00    | Before anything was posted  |
| 2024-01-05 | Assets:Checking | 1000.00 | The date itself is included |
| 2024-02-28 | Assets:Checking | 955.00  |                             |
| 2024-12-31 | Assets:Checking | 900.00  | Everything                  |

Attributes DatedAccountEntry
Description An AccountEntry with the date the transaction behind it was posted
\ on, for scenarios about when an account was last used.
| Attribute | Type        | Default    |
| Date      | Date        | 2024-01-01 |
| Account   | AccountPath |            |
| Amount    | Money       | 0.00       |

Attributes BalanceAsAt
| Attribute | Type        | Default    |
| AsAt      | Date        | 2024-01-01 |
| Account   | AccountPath |            |
| Balance   | Money       | 0.00       |
| Notes     | String      | none       |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Opening balances
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Scenario Opening a real account with a balance
Description There is no opening balance field on an account. An opening balance
\ is a transaction against Equity:Opening Balances, so the book
\ balances from its first day. This is what lets an import of twenty
\ years of history start from a known position.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
When account added with an opening balance : AccountWithOpeningBalance Vertical
| Path           | Assets:Brokerage Cash |
| Type           | Checking              |
| OpeningBalance | 2500.00               |
| OpeningDate    | 2005-01-01            |
Then transactions created are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount         | Date       | Destination             | Amount  |
| T1  | Assets:Brokerage Cash | 2005-01-01 | Equity:Opening Balances | 2500.00 |

Attributes AccountWithOpeningBalance
| Attribute      | Type        | Default    |
| Path           | AccountPath |            |
| Type           | AccountType | Checking   |
| OpeningBalance | Money       | 0.00       |
| OpeningDate    | Date        | 2005-01-01 |
| Bank           | Text        | none       |
| AccountNumber  | Text        | none       |

Scenario Opening a credit card with a balance owed
Description The opening balance is entered the way the statement reads, as an
\ amount owed, and is stored with the sign the transaction needs.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
When account added with an opening balance : AccountWithOpeningBalance Vertical
| Path           | Liabilities:Amex |
| Type           | CreditCard       |
| OpeningBalance | 300.00           |
| OpeningDate    | 2005-01-01       |
Then transactions created are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount    | Date       | Destination             | Amount  |
| T1  | Liabilities:Amex | 2005-01-01 | Equity:Opening Balances | -300.00 |
Then balances are : AccountBalance
| Account          | RawBalance | DisplayBalance | Notes            |
| Liabilities:Amex | -300.00    | 300.00         | Owed, as entered |

Scenario An account opened with no balance posts nothing
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
When account added with an opening balance : AccountWithOpeningBalance Vertical
| Path           | Assets:Money Market |
| Type           | Checking            |
| OpeningBalance | 0.00                |
| OpeningDate    | 2005-01-01          |
Then transactions created are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount | Date | Destination | Amount |
# No rows: an opening balance of zero is not worth a transaction.

Scenario An account is opened with its bank and the number on the statement
Description The dialog that opens an account asks for both, rather than
\ leaving them to a second visit to the settings dialog. They are
\ what a person has in front of them at that moment -- the statement
\ is on the desk, which is usually why the account is being opened at
\ all -- and an account carrying its number from the first day is one
\ the first download can place without asking. See what a download
\ teaches an account in ImportOfx, which is the other way they are
\ filled in.
Details Neither is required. An account opened with no bank and no number is
\ what every account used to be, and the settings dialog is still where
\ one is labelled afterwards. The number is kept exactly as typed,
\ which is the account-number rule above and not a second decision
\ taken here.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
When account added with an opening balance : AccountWithOpeningBalance Vertical
| Path           | Assets:Second Checking |
| Type           | Checking               |
| OpeningBalance | 0.00                   |
| OpeningDate    | 2005-01-01             |
| Bank           | First Bank             |
| AccountNumber  | 0012-3456              |
Then account settings are : AccountSettings Vertical
| Path          | Assets:Second Checking |
| Bank          | First Bank             |
| BankId        | none                   |
| AccountNumber | 0012-3456              |
# BankId is not asked for and stays empty. Nobody types a routing number to
# label an account; the first download writes it down.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Changing the type of an account
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Scenario The type of an unused account may be changed
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
Given Expenses:Auto:Repairs has entries : EntryCount
| Count |
| 0     |
When account type changed : AccountTypeChange Vertical
| Path    | Expenses:Auto:Repairs |
| NewType | Income                |
Then account is : Account Vertical CompareOnly
| Path | Expenses:Auto:Repairs |
| Type | Income                |

Attributes AccountTypeChange
| Attribute | Type        | Default |
| Path      | AccountPath |         |
| NewType   | AccountType | Checking |

Scenario Changing type across the real and nominal divide is refused
Description Changing an Expense into a Bank would change the sign that every
\ existing transaction is read with, and so rewrite history in silence.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
Given Expenses:Groceries has entries : EntryCount
| Count |
| 412   |
When account type changed : AccountTypeChange Vertical
| Path    | Expenses:Groceries |
| NewType | Checking           |
Then rejected because : Rejection
| Reason                                                               |
| Expenses:Groceries has postings; a category cannot become an account |

Scenario Changing type within the real accounts is allowed
Description A Bank mistakenly set up as Cash is a common correction, and the
\ display sign is the same for both, so no transaction changes meaning.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
Given Assets:Savings has entries : EntryCount
| Count |
| 96    |
When account type changed : AccountTypeChange Vertical
| Path    | Assets:Savings |
| NewType | Cash           |
Then account is : Account Vertical CompareOnly
| Path | Assets:Savings |
| Type | Cash           |
